  "notes": "The lane's analog classicist, and the entry says outright that \"the 60s-Motown template runs faster than the Soulquarians one\" — 86–125 for the retro-soul material and 72–95 for the ballads, which is the widest straight band in this batch. The drum block is the stated 60s tracking aesthetic: \"tight, dry, high-tuned kit recorded close and compressed\", \"kick on 1 and 3, snare hard on 2&4 with a wooden crack, tambourine doubling the backbeat, and no 16th hat carpet; the groove is carried by bass and guitar, not by the hats\". So the kick anchors are literally 1 and 3, syncopation is the lowest in this batch, the hat base is an 8th at a fill density of 0.14, the snare sits on the grid with a rimshot layer, the velocity tiers are the hardest here, and the tambourine mirrors the clap. Harmony is the entry's Motown diatonicism — the two families it writes out — with the triad weight raised far above the lane's to match \"gospel 6ths and blues sevenths\" rather than m9 stacks. The melodic claim is short, hooky, mid-register with a light rasp, arranged in tight 60s-group thirds. Bass carries the entry's strongest statement — \"bass is the compositional voice… melodic, Jamerson-lineage 8th-and-16th lines with chromatic approach notes, high-register fills, and the bass entering before the drums\" — so the root probability is low, the register climbs to 50, and the chord voicing was pushed up to 52 to stay above it. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum probability and velocity band and all melody/countermelody numbers. ⚠ The research itself marks the tempo band [LOW-CONF] as genre inference, and `confidence` is medium for that reason.",
